# Fuller versions for Latin America 1886 onward (override rw_la)
R = {
"Cuba ends slavery": ("PE",2,"Spain finally abolished slavery in Cuba, its richest remaining colony.","Cuba's sugar boom had depended on enslaved Africans. The Ten Years' War (1868–78), in which many enslaved people fought for independence, and pressure from abolitionists forced Spain to end slavery in stages.","Formerly enslaved Cubans joined the next independence war in large numbers.","Afro-Cuban music, religion and culture are at the heart of Cuban identity."),
"Brazil becomes a republic": ("P",2,"Brazil's army overthrew Emperor Pedro II and declared a republic.","Planters angry about abolition without compensation, and officers who wanted more power, backed a nearly bloodless coup. The emperor sailed into exile.","The military became a powerful player in Brazilian politics for the next century.","Brazil's flag motto, 'Order and Progress', comes from this republic's founders."),
"Cuban War of Independence": ("WP",2,"Cubans rose once more against Spain, led by the poet José Martí.","Martí, who had spent years in exile in New York, returned to fight and was killed in one of the first battles. Spain herded civilians into camps where many died.","News of Spanish brutality, hyped by US newspapers, pulled the US into the war in 1898.","Martí is Cuba's national hero, honored by both Castro's government and Cuban exiles."),
"Spain loses Cuba & Puerto Rico": ("P",2,"Spain lost the last of its American empire, and the US took its place.","After the Spanish-American War, Cuba became nominally independent under heavy US control, and Puerto Rico became a US territory.","The US kept the right to intervene in Cuba and built the Guantánamo Bay naval base.","Puerto Rico is still a US territory whose residents can't vote for president."),
"Panama splits from Colombia": ("P",2,"The US backed Panama's independence so it could build a canal.","When Colombia's senate rejected a canal treaty, Theodore Roosevelt sent warships to stop Colombian troops from crushing a Panamanian revolt. Panama quickly signed the deal Colombia had refused.","The US controlled the Canal Zone for most of the 20th century.","Panama took full control of the canal in 1999."),
"Roosevelt Corollary": ("P",2,"The US declared itself the policeman of the Western Hemisphere.","Worried Europeans would seize Latin American ports over unpaid debts, Theodore Roosevelt said the US would step in instead.","US Marines occupied Haiti, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua for years at a time.","It still shapes Latin American suspicion of US power."),
"Mexican Revolution": ("WPI",3,"Mexico's revolution was the first great social revolution of the 20th century.","It began as a revolt against Porfirio Díaz's 35-year rule and became a decade of civil war among leaders like Emiliano Zapata, who demanded 'land and liberty', and Pancho Villa. About 1–2 million died.","The 1917 constitution promised land reform, workers' rights and national ownership of resources.","The party that emerged from the revolution ruled Mexico for 71 years."),
"Panama Canal opens": ("ES",3,"The Panama Canal joined the Atlantic and Pacific oceans.","France had failed after about 22,000 workers died of disease. The US controlled mosquitoes that spread malaria and yellow fever and built huge locks that lift ships 26 meters.","A voyage from New York to San Francisco was cut by about 8,000 miles.","About 5% of world seaborne trade passes through the canal, and droughts now threaten its water supply."),
"Vargas takes power in Brazil": ("PE",2,"Getúlio Vargas took power and built modern Brazil.","Vargas led a revolt in 1930 and ruled as president and dictator. He built steel mills, created labor laws and a minimum wage, and later founded the oil company Petrobras.","Overthrown in 1945, he was elected again in 1950 and killed himself in office in 1954.","He's remembered as 'the father of the poor' and the founder of Brazil's state-led development."),
"Mexico nationalizes oil": ("EP",3,"Mexico seized foreign oil companies and made oil a symbol of national pride.","After US and British companies refused to obey a court ruling on workers' wages, President Lázaro Cárdenas nationalized them. Mexicans donated jewelry and chickens to help pay compensation.","State company Pemex controlled Mexican oil for decades.","Resource nationalism, from Iran to Venezuela to Bolivia, followed Mexico's example."),
"Perón in Argentina": ("P",2,"Juan Perón built a movement that still dominates Argentine politics.","An army colonel, Perón won workers' loyalty with higher wages, pensions and union rights. His wife Eva, 'Evita', became an icon of the poor.","He was overthrown in 1955, returned in 1973, and died in office a year later.","Peronism, hard to label left or right, is still Argentina's biggest political force."),
"US-backed coup in Guatemala": ("PW",2,"The CIA overthrew Guatemala's elected president to protect a US fruit company.","President Jacobo Árbenz's land reform took unused land from the United Fruit Company. The US branded him communist and backed an invasion by exiles.","Guatemala fell into a 36-year civil war that killed about 200,000, mostly Indigenous Maya.","Che Guevara was in Guatemala at the time; it convinced him that change needed armed revolution."),
"Cuban Revolution": ("PWI",3,"Fidel Castro's guerrillas took power in Cuba, creating a communist state 90 miles from the US.","Castro and Che Guevara led a small rebel army from the Sierra Maestra mountains. Dictator Fulgencio Batista fled on New Year's Day 1959.","Castro nationalized US businesses and allied with the Soviet Union; over a million Cubans eventually emigrated.","The US embargo on Cuba continues today."),
"Bay of Pigs": ("W",2,"A CIA-backed invasion of Cuba collapsed in three days.","About 1,400 Cuban exiles trained by the CIA landed at the Bay of Pigs. Kennedy refused to send air support, and Castro's forces captured most of them.","Castro declared Cuba socialist and moved closer to Moscow, which led to the missile crisis.","It's a classic case study in bad decision-making ('groupthink')."),
"Cuban Missile Crisis": ("WP",3,"For 13 days, the world came closer to nuclear war than ever before.","Khrushchev secretly placed nuclear missiles in Cuba. Kennedy blockaded the island. Khrushchev removed them in exchange for a US promise not to invade Cuba, and a secret deal to remove US missiles from Turkey.","Both sides set up a hotline and signed a nuclear test-ban treaty.","It's the textbook example of crisis diplomacy."),
"Brazil military coup": ("P",2,"Brazil's army overthrew its president and ruled for 21 years.","Fearing leftist reforms, the military, with US support, ousted João Goulart. The regime used torture and censorship but also oversaw an economic boom.","Brazil returned to democracy in 1985.","Former president Dilma Rousseff was tortured as a young activist under the regime."),
"Che killed in Bolivia": ("WP",2,"Che Guevara was captured and executed in the Bolivian jungle.","Che left Cuba to spread revolution and tried to start a guerrilla war in Bolivia. Peasants didn't join him, and Bolivian troops with CIA help caught him.","His death turned him into a global icon of rebellion.","The famous photo of Che is one of the most reproduced images in the world."),
"Pinochet coup in Chile": ("PWE",3,"Chile's army overthrew elected socialist Salvador Allende on September 11, 1973.","Allende's government nationalized copper and faced shortages and US-backed pressure. General Augusto Pinochet bombed the presidential palace; Allende died inside.","Pinochet's regime killed or disappeared over 3,000 people and turned Chile into a free-market laboratory.","Chile's debates over its constitution still revolve around the Pinochet legacy."),
"Argentina's Dirty War": ("PW",2,"Argentina's military dictatorship 'disappeared' up to 30,000 people.","The junta kidnapped students, unionists and suspected leftists, torturing many in secret centers. Some were thrown alive from planes into the sea.","The Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo marched every week demanding answers; the junta fell after the Falklands War.","Trials of officers continue, and grandmothers are still finding stolen grandchildren through DNA."),
"Sandinista revolution": ("PW",2,"Nicaragua's Sandinistas overthrew the Somoza family dictatorship.","The Somozas had ruled for over 40 years with US support. A popular uprising led by the Sandinistas toppled them.","The US backed the Contra rebels in a war that killed about 30,000; the Iran-Contra scandal followed.","Sandinista leader Daniel Ortega has ruled Nicaragua again since 2007, increasingly as an authoritarian."),
"Falklands War; debt crisis": ("WE",2,"Argentina lost a war over the Falklands, and Latin America fell into a debt crisis.","Argentina's junta invaded the British-ruled Falklands to boost its popularity. Britain retook them in 10 weeks. The same year, Mexico announced it couldn't pay its debts, setting off a crisis across the region.","The junta collapsed; Latin America's 1980s became the 'lost decade'.","Argentina still claims the islands it calls the Malvinas."),
"US invades Panama": ("W",1,"The US invaded Panama to arrest its leader, a former CIA ally.","Manuel Noriega had worked with the CIA but became a drug trafficker. US troops captured him and took him to Miami for trial.","Hundreds of Panamanian civilians were killed.","It was the last large-scale US invasion in Latin America."),
"NAFTA; Zapatista uprising": ("PE",2,"On the day NAFTA began, Indigenous rebels rose up in southern Mexico.","The Zapatistas, named after Emiliano Zapata, seized towns in Chiapas, saying free trade would destroy Indigenous farmers.","After a ceasefire, they ran self-governing communities in Chiapas.","Their masked spokesman Subcomandante Marcos inspired global anti-globalization movements."),
"Chávez in Venezuela": ("PE",2,"Hugo Chávez took power and launched Latin America's 'pink tide' to the left.","A former paratrooper who had tried a coup in 1992, Chávez won the election and used oil money for social programs and anti-US politics.","Leftist leaders won across the region, from Bolivia to Brazil.","Venezuela's later collapse under his successor Maduro is often blamed on his policies."),
"Argentina defaults": ("E",2,"Argentina defaulted on about $100 billion of debt.","Argentina had pegged its peso to the dollar. When the peg broke, banks froze accounts, riots broke out and the country had five presidents in two weeks.","Poverty soared above 50%.","Argentina has defaulted on its debt nine times in its history."),
"Lula in Brazil": ("PE",2,"A former shoeshine boy and union leader became Brazil's president.","Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva's Bolsa Família program paid poor families to keep children in school, lifting millions from poverty during a commodity boom.","He was later jailed for corruption, freed when the conviction was annulled, and re-elected in 2022.","He's one of Latin America's most influential leaders."),
"Haiti earthquake": ("S",2,"An earthquake destroyed much of Haiti's capital and killed over 200,000 people.","The quake struck near Port-au-Prince, where poorly built concrete buildings collapsed.","Billions in aid poured in, but much never reached Haitians; UN peacekeepers brought cholera.","Haiti still hasn't recovered."),
"Venezuela crisis deepens": ("E",2,"Venezuela's economy collapsed, and millions fled.","When oil prices crashed in 2014, Venezuela, which relied on oil for most exports, ran out of money. Hyperinflation and shortages followed.","Nearly 8 million Venezuelans have left the country.","It's one of the largest displacement crises in the world."),
"Colombia-FARC peace deal": ("PW",2,"Colombia made peace with the FARC guerrillas after 52 years of war.","President Juan Manuel Santos negotiated in Havana. Voters narrowly rejected the first deal; a revised one passed Congress.","About 13,000 FARC members disarmed and the group became a political party.","Santos won the Nobel Peace Prize; violence by other armed groups continues."),
"Haiti's president assassinated": ("P",2,"Mercenaries killed Haiti's president in his bedroom.","Jovenel Moïse was shot by a team that included Colombian ex-soldiers.","Gangs took over most of Port-au-Prince.","Haiti's political and security crisis continues."),
"Milei elected in Argentina": ("PE",2,"A libertarian economist won Argentina's presidency promising to take a chainsaw to the state.","Javier Milei won on inflation above 100%.","He cut spending sharply and brought monthly inflation down.","His experiment is closely watched worldwide."),
}
